  "content": {
    "id": "p141-q37-content",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $f$ and $g$ be odd and even functions respectively. Show that:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p141-q37-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(f(x))$ is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p141-q37-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "False in general. ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f\\circ f",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is odd and need not be even. For ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(x)=x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(f(x))=x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is not even.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p141-q37-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Substitute ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "-x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and apply oddness to the inner and outer functions.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The printed assertion is false in general. Since $f$ is odd,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(f(-x))&=f(-f(x))\\\\f(f(-x))&=-f(f(x)).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe composition domain is symmetric about $0$: whenever $x$ and $f(x)$ belong to the domain of $f$, so do $-x$ and $-f(x)$. Thus $f\\circ f$ is odd on its composition domain.\n\nFor a counterexample to evenness, take $f(x)=x$ on $\\mathbb R$. Then $f(f(x))=x$, which is not even. This satisfies the stated oddness assumption on $f$ and is compatible with any even $g$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-q37-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$g(g(x))$ is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p141-q37-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "False in general. ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "g\\circ g",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is even and need not be odd. For ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "g(x)=x^2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "g(g(x))=x^4",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is not odd.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p141-q37-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "g(-x)=g(x)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " for the inner function.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The printed assertion is false in general. Since $g$ is even,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}g(g(-x))&=g(g(x)).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe composition domain is symmetric about $0$: at $x$ and $-x$, the inner function gives the same intermediate value. Thus $g\\circ g$ is even on its composition domain.\n\nFor a counterexample to oddness, take $g(x)=x^2$ on $\\mathbb R$. Then $g(g(x))=x^4$, which is not odd: its values at $1$ and $-1$ are both $1$. This satisfies the stated evenness assumption on $g$ and is compatible with any odd $f$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-q37-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(g(x))$ and $f(f(x))$ are both even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p141-q37-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "False in general. ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f\\circ g",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is even; ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f\\circ f",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is odd and need not be even. Taking ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(x)=x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "g(x)=x^2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " gives ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(g(x))=x^2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(f(x))=x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p141-q37-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Substitute ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "-x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " into each composite and apply the relevant parity identities.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The first assertion is true. Since $g$ is even,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(-x))&=f(g(x)).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe domain of $f\\circ g$ is symmetric because the inner values at $x$ and $-x$ are equal. Hence $f\\circ g$ is even.\n\nFor the second composite, oddness of $f$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(f(-x))&=f(-f(x))\\\\f(f(-x))&=-f(f(x)).\\end{align*}$$\n\nIts composition domain is also symmetric, since the domain of $f$ is symmetric and $f(-x)=-f(x)$. Hence $f\\circ f$ is odd, but it need not be even.\n\nTake $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=x^2$ on $\\mathbb R$. Then\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=x^2,\\\\f(f(x))&=x.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe first is even and the second is not. Therefore the printed claim that both are even is false in general.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
